<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
  <title>Section 2.2: The Unit Circle</title>

  <p>
    <alert>MTH 124</alert>
  </p>

  <sidebyside widths="25% 70%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        The unit circle, that is a circle centered at the origin with radius 1.
        A point on the circle has an x and y coordinate, which creates a right
        triangle with the x-axis with a horizontal length of |x| and a vertical
        leg of |y| and the hypotenuse of 1.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2">
        <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
          <definition>
            a=cos(0.9)
          </definition>

          <definition>
            b=sin(0.9)
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.2000,-1.2,1.2,1.2)">
            <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
            <circle radius="1" center="(0,0)"/>
            <point p="(a,b)">
              <m>
                (x,y)
              </m>
            </point>
            <line endpoints="((0,0), (a,b))" thickness="5" dash="4 4" stroke="blue" offset="(0,10)"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0), (a,0))" stroke="blue" dash="4 4" thickness="5" offset="(0,-20)"><m>x</m></line>
            <line endpoints="((a,0), (a,b))" stroke="blue" dash="4 4" thickness="5"/>
            <label anchor="(.25,.5)">
              <m>
                1
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(.73,.3)">
              <m>
                y
              </m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <p>
      The <term>unit circle</term> is a circle of radius <m>1</m> that is
      centered at the origin, <m>(0,0)</m>.
      A point on the circle has some coordinates <m>(x,y)</m>, and we can form a
      right triangle with the <m>x</m>-axis where the horizontal leg has length
      <m>|x|</m>, the vertical leg has length <m>|y|</m>, and the hypotenuse
      (the radius of the circle) is length 1.
      Pythagorean Theorem then tells us that
      <md>
        x^2 + y^2 = 1!
      </md>
      This means we can determine some coordinates <q>nicely.</q>
    </p>
  </sidebyside>
  

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.1</title>

    <statement>
      <sidebyside widths="35% 63%">
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                We start at the right side of the circle <m>(1,0)</m> and travel
                around it counter-clockwise.
                Label that point <m>t=0</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The circumference of the circle is <m>2\pi</m>, so halfway
                around the circle is <m>\pi</m>.
                Label that point <m>t=\pi</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Similarly, we can divide the circumference ( <m>2\pi</m> )
                nicely into 24 pieces, each with a length of
                <m>\frac{\pi}{12}</m> around the edge of the circle.
                Fill them in (simplify the fractions as much as possible, so
                <m>\frac{2\pi}{12} = \frac{\pi}{6} </m>,
                <m>\frac{3\pi}{12} = \frac{\pi}{4},  \ldots</m>.)
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>

        <image>
          <shortdescription>
            The unit circle, with 24 equally spaced points around the perimeter.
            The first two above the x-axis are labeled, t=pi/12 and t=pi/6.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-1">
            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
              <definition>
                f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-1.3,1.3,1.3)">
                <grid-axes decorations="no" spacings="((-10,1/5,10),(-10,1/5,10))"/>
                <circle radius="1" center="(0,0)"/>
                <repeat parameter="k=0..24">
                  <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
                </repeat>
                <label anchor="f(1)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)">
                  <m>
                    t=\frac{\pi}{12}
                  </m>
                </label>
                <label anchor="f(2)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)">
                  <m>
                    t=\frac{\pi}{6}
                  </m>
                </label>
              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
      </sidebyside>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <image width="60%">
          <shortdescription>
            The unit circle, with 24 equally spaced points around the perimeter.
            The first two above the x-axis are labeled, t=pi/12 and t=pi/6.
          </shortdescription>
          <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-1soln">
            <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
              <definition>
                f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
              </definition>
              <definition>
                alignments=['e','e','e','e','ne','ne','ne','nw', 'nw', 'nw', 'w','w','nw', 'w', 'w', 'sw','sw','sw','sw','s','se','se','se','e']
              </definition>
              <definition substitution="no">
                labels=['0',r'\frac{\pi}{12}',r'\frac{\pi}{6}',r'\frac{\pi}{4}',r'\frac{\pi}{3}',r'\frac{5\pi}{12}',
                r'\frac{\pi}{2}',r'\frac{7\pi}{12}', r'\frac{2\pi}{3}',r'\frac{3\pi}{4}',r'\frac{5\pi}{6}', r'\frac{11\pi}{12}', 
                r'\pi', r'\frac{13\pi}{12}', r'\frac{7\pi}{6}', r'\frac{5\pi}{4}', r'\frac{4\pi}{3}', 
                r'\frac{17\pi}{12}', r'\frac{3\pi}{2}', 
                r'\frac{19\pi}{12}', r'\frac{5\pi}{3}', r'\frac{7\pi}{4}', r'\frac{11\pi}{6}', 
                r'\frac{23\pi}{12}']
              </definition>
              <coordinates bbox="(-1.3,-1.3,1.3,1.3)">
                <grid-axes decorations="no" spacings="((-10,1/5,10),(-10,1/5,10))"/>
                <circle radius="1" center="(0,0)"/>
            
                <repeat parameter="k=0..24">
                  <point at="point" p="f(k)" alignment="alignments[k]">
                    <m>${labels[k]}</m>
                  </point>
                </repeat>

              </coordinates>
            </diagram>
          </prefigure>
        </image>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <definition>
    <title>Definition 2.2.5</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        An angle whose vertex is at the center of a circle measures
        <term>1 radian</term> provided the arc the angle intercepts on the
        circle equals the radius of the circle.
      </p>

      <p>
        Which just means: the distance along the edge of the circle is the
        <fillin/> as the angle between the <m>x-</m> axis and the point on the
        circle!
        (This is why we use radians...)
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Converting between radians and degrees</title>

    <p>
      <md>
        1 \text{ radian }= \frac{180}{\pi}^{\circ},  
      \quad \quad \quad \quad 1^{\circ} = \frac{\pi}{180} \text{ radians}
      </md>
      Equivalent to 
      <md>
        \pi \text{ radians } = 180^{\circ}
      </md>
      
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <example workspace="1.1in">
    <p>
      (We'll do as a class:)
    </p>

    <p>
      Convert <m>\pi</m> radians to degrees.
      Convert <m>90</m> degrees to radians.
    </p>
  </example>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.2. Converting between radians and degrees</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Convert each of the following quantities to the alternative measure:
        degrees to radians or radians to degrees.
      </p>

      <p>
        <ol cols="3" marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>30^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>\frac{2\pi}{3}</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>\frac{5\pi}{4}</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>240^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>17^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p workspace="1.6in">
              <m>2</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-2-soln">
      <p>
        <ol cols="3" marker="a.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>30^{\circ}\left(\frac{\pi}{180}\right) = \frac{\pi}{6}</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(\frac{2\pi}{3}\right)\left(\frac{180}\pi\right) = 120^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\left(\frac{5\pi}{4}\right)\left(\frac{180}\pi\right) = 225^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>240^{\circ}\left(\frac{\pi}{180}\right) = \frac{4\pi}{3}</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>17^{\circ}\left(\frac{\pi}{180}\right) = \frac{17\pi}{180}</m> radians
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>2\left(\frac{180}\pi\right) = \left(\frac{360}{\pi}\right)^\circ</m>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </activity>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.3 Special points on the unit circle</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In what follows, we work to understand key relationships in
        <m>45^{\circ} - 45^{\circ} - 90^{\circ}</m> and
        <m>30^{\circ} - 60^{\circ} - 90^{\circ}</m> right triangles.
        (This will help us find exact coordinates on the unit circle.)
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="40%  60%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A right trianglewith angles of 45, 45, and 90 degrees with
              hypotenus 1, and side lengths x and y.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2),(sqrt(2)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/4,sqrt(2)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((0,0),(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2),(sqrt(2)/2,0))" offset="(10,-6)">
                    <m>
                      45^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(2)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2))" offset="(1,-6)">
                    <m>
                      45^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p>
            For the <m>45^{\circ} - 45^{\circ} - 90^{\circ}</m> triangle with
            legs of length <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> and hypotenuse of length 1,
            because the triangle is an isosceles triangle, what is the
            relationship between <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>?
            Use this fact to simplify the Pythagorean Theorem
            <m>x^2+y^2 = 1</m>- what are the exact values of <m>x</m> and
            <m>y</m>?
            (Exact means leave <m>\pi</m> 's or square roots or fractions in
            your answer so you do not round!)
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>x=y</m> on isosceles triangles, so Pythagorean Theorem says 
          <m>{x^2 + x^2 = 1}</m>, or <m>{2x^2 = 1}</m>.  This means 
          <m>{x^2 = \frac12}</m> and <m>{x = \sqrt{\frac12} = \frac{\sqrt{1}}{\sqrt{2}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}=\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2}}</m>.  (Since <m>x</m>
          is a length we don't need to include <m>x=\pm\sqrt{\frac12}</m>).  Then 
          <m>{x = y = \frac{\sqrt 2}{2}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="40% 60%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A right triangle with angles of 30, 60, 90 degrees with hypotenus
              1 and side lengths x and y.
              In the picture, the side length of x is adjacent to the 30 degree
              angle.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3-b">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="5">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2),(sqrt(3)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/4,sqrt(1)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2),(sqrt(3)/2,0))" offset="(10,-6)">
                    <m>
                      60^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(3)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2))" offset="(1,3)">
                    <m>
                      30^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p>
            Now consider the <m>30^{\circ} - 60^{\circ} - 90^{\circ}</m>
            triangle with hypotenuse of length 1 and the longer leg (of length
            <m>x</m> ) lying along the positive <m>x</m>-axis.
            Reflect this triangle across the -axis, what special triangle is
            formed (look at the angles)?
            Can we use this to find the exact length of <m>y</m>?
            Once we have <m>y</m>, go back and find the exact value of <m>x</m>
            using <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>.
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          With the reflection we have an equilateral triangle with all sides 
          equal to 1!  Since <m>y</m> is half of the length of this doubled triangle
          then <m>{y=\frac12}</m>.  Then Pythagorean Theorem: <m>{x^2+(\frac12)^2 = 1}</m>. 
          That is <m>{x^2 + \frac14 = 1}</m> or <m>{x^2 = \frac34}</m> or 
          <m>{x = \frac{\sqrt 3}{\sqrt 4} = \frac{\sqrt 3}{4}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <sidebyside widths="40% 60%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A right triangle with angles of 30, 60, 90 degrees with hypotenus
              1 and side lengths x and y.
              In the picture, the side length of x is adjacent to the 60 degree
              angle.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3-c">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2),(sqrt(1)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/4,sqrt(3)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((0,0),(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2),(sqrt(1)/2,0))" offset="(-5,-10)">
                    <m>
                      30^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(1)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2))" offset="(0,0)">
                    <m>
                      60^\circ
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <p>
            Note we can take the same
            <m>30^{\circ} - 60^{\circ} - 90^{\circ}</m> and flip it so <m>y</m>
            corresponds to the smaller, horizontal leg.
            What are the <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> values in this case?
            (Hint, by looking at (b) you can save yourself a lot of work!)
          </p>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          Switch the <m>x, y</m>!  That is <m>{x = \frac12, \ y=\frac{\sqrt 3}{2}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="0.01in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          We know from the conversion factor from degrees to radians that an
          angle of <m>30^{\circ}</m> corresponds to an angle measuring
          <m>\frac{\pi}{6}</m> radians, <m>45^{\circ}</m> corresponds to
          <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m> radians, and <m>60^{\circ}</m> corresponds to
          <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m> radians.
          Use parts (a) - (c) to label the coordinate points <m>(x,y)</m> with
          the exact values of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> on the three triangles
          below.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%">
          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A central angle on the unit circle with angle pi/6 or 30 degrees.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3-d">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                  <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2),(sqrt(3)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes" thickness="3"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/4,sqrt(1)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(3)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2))" offset="(0,2)">
                    <m>
                      \pi / 6
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <point p="(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2)"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A central angle on the unit circle with angle pi/4 or 45 degrees.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3-e">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                  <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2),(sqrt(2)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes" thickness="3"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/4,sqrt(2)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(2)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2))" offset="(0,-4)">
                    <m>
                      \pi / 4
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <point p="(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2)"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>

          <image>
            <shortdescription>
              A central angle on the unit circle with angle pi/3 or 60 degrees.
            </shortdescription>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-3-f">
              <diagram dimensions="(250,250)" margins="30">
                <coordinates bbox="(-.3,-.3,1,1)">
                  <grid-axes decorations="no"/>
                  <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
                  <triangle vertices="((0,0),(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2),(sqrt(1)/2,0))" closed="yes" stroke="blue" angle-markers="yes" thickness="3"/>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/4,0)" alignment="s">
                    <m>
                      x
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/4)" alignment="e">
                    <m>
                      y
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/4,sqrt(3)/4)" alignment="nw">
                    <m>
                      1
                    </m>
                  </label>
                  <angle-marker points="((sqrt(1)/2,0),(0,0),(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2))" offset="(0,0)">
                    <m>
                      \pi/3
                    </m>
                  </angle-marker>
                  <point p="(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2)"/>
                </coordinates>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          The first picture corresponds the part b and so knowing we have 
          the coordinate with the values of <m>(x,y)</m> with the <m>x,y</m> for that 
          triangle, then the point is at the coordinate <m>(\frac{\sqrt 3}{2}, \frac12)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second picture corresponds the part a and so knowing we have 
          the coordinate with the values of <m>(x,y)</m> with the <m>x,y</m> for that 
          triangle, then the point is at the coordinate <m>(\frac{\sqrt 2}{2}, \frac{\sqrt 2}2)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The third picture corresponds the part c and so knowing we have 
          the coordinate with the values of <m>(x,y)</m> with the <m>x,y</m> for that 
          triangle, then the point is at the coordinate <m>(\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt 3}2)</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <sidebyside widths="60% 28%">
    <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A unit circle with some coordinate points filled in.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-44">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="20">
          <definition>
            f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,1.5,1.5)">
            <grid-axes decorations="no" spacings="((-10,1/5,10),(-10,1/5,10))"/>
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
            <repeat parameter="k=2..4">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=8..10">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=14..16">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=20..22">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <point p="f(0)"/>
            <point p="f(6)"/>
            <point p="f(12)"/>
            <point p="f(18)"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,1/2))" stroke="blue" dash="4 4"/>
            <label anchor="(.4,.35)">
              <m>
                1
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2)" alignment="e">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac12), t=\frac{\pi}{6}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}2), t=\frac{\pi}{4}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}2), t=\frac{\pi}{3}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(4)/2,sqrt(0)/2)" alignment="e">
              <m>
                (1,0), t=0
              </m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>

    <p>
      The coordinates are called <term>terminal points.</term> The far right of
      the circle we know is terminal point <m>(1,0)</m> because the radius is 1
      and we are on the <m>x</m>-axis.
      Since we start on the far right of the circle, that corresponds to angle
      <m>t=0</m>.
      Similarly the point at the top of the circle is the terminal point
      <m>(0,1)</m>, corresponding to <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
      Fill in those points in the graph.
      Then use the previous activity (and symmetry of the coordinates) to fill
      in the other points on the unit circle.
      We have 16 specials points total whose location we can determine exactly!
    </p>
  </sidebyside>

  <solution component="S2-2-soln">
     <image>
      <shortdescription>
        A unit circle with some coordinate points filled in.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-124-2-2-44soln">
        <diagram dimensions="(500,500)" margins="20">
          <definition>
            f(t)=(cos(t*pi/12),sin(t*pi/12))
          </definition>
          <coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.5,1.5,1.5)">
            <grid-axes decorations="no" spacings="((-10,1/5,10),(-10,1/5,10))"/>
            <circle center="(0,0)" radius="1" stroke="blue"/>
            <repeat parameter="k=2..4">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=8..10">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=14..16">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <repeat parameter="k=20..22">
              <point at="point" p="f(k)"/>
            </repeat>
            <point p="f(0)"/>
            <point p="f(6)"/>
            <point p="f(12)"/>
            <point p="f(18)"/>
            <line endpoints="((0,0),(sqrt(3)/2,1/2))" stroke="blue" dash="4 4"/>
            <label anchor="(.4,.35)">
              <m>
                1
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2)" alignment="e">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac12), t=\frac{\pi}{6}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}2), t=\frac{\pi}{4}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}2), t=\frac{\pi}{3}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(4)/2,sqrt(0)/2)" alignment="ne">
              <m>
                (1,0), t=0
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(0)/2,sqrt(4)/2)" alignment="ne">
              <m>
                (0,1), t=\frac{\pi}{2}
              </m>
            </label>

            <label anchor="(-sqrt(3)/2,sqrt(1)/2)" alignment="w">
              <m>
                (-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},\frac12), t=\frac{5\pi}{6}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-sqrt(2)/2,sqrt(2)/2)" alignment="w" offset="(-3,0)">
              <m>
                (-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},\frac{\sqrt{2}}2), t=\frac{3\pi}{4}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-sqrt(1)/2,sqrt(3)/2)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,0)">
              <m>
                (-\frac{1}{2},\frac{\sqrt{3}}2), t=\frac{2\pi}{3}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-sqrt(4)/2,sqrt(0)/2)" alignment="nw">
              <m>
                (-1,0), t=\pi
              </m>
            </label>

            <label anchor="(-sqrt(3)/2,-sqrt(1)/2)" alignment="w">
              <m>
                (-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},-\frac12), t=\frac{7\pi}{6}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-sqrt(2)/2,-sqrt(2)/2)" alignment="w" offset="(-3,0)">
              <m>
                (-\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},-\frac{\sqrt{2}}2), t=\frac{5\pi}{4}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(-sqrt(1)/2,-sqrt(3)/2)" alignment="w" offset="(-8,0)">
              <m>
                (-\frac{1}{2},\frac{-\sqrt{3}}2), t=\frac{4\pi}{3}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(0,-1)" alignment="sw">
              <m>
                (0,-1), t=\pi
              </m>
            </label>

            <label anchor="(sqrt(3)/2,-sqrt(1)/2)" alignment="e">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2},-\frac12), t=\frac{11\pi}{6}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(2)/2,-sqrt(2)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(3,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{\sqrt{2}}{2},-\frac{\sqrt{2}}2), t=\frac{7\pi}{4}
              </m>
            </label>
            <label anchor="(sqrt(1)/2,-sqrt(3)/2)" alignment="e" offset="(8,0)">
              <m>
                (\frac{1}{2},-\frac{\sqrt{3}}2), t=\frac{5\pi}{3}
              </m>
            </label>
          </coordinates>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>


  </solution>

  <assemblage>
    <title>Connecting arc length and angles in non-unit circles</title>

    <p>
      In a circle of radius <m>r</m>, a central angle measuring <m>\theta</m>
      radians intercepts an arc of the circle, and the arc has length <m>s</m>
      where
      <md>
        s=r\theta.
      </md>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <activity>
    <title>Activity 2.2.4 Non-unit circles</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In a circle of radius <m>r</m>, each point of the unit circle is a
        distance <m>r</m> further away from the origin.
        So <m>(1,0)</m> on the unit circle would correspond to <m>(r,0)</m>, and
        so on.
        That is, each terminal point on the unit circle corresponds to the same
        point, except each coordinate is multiplied by the new radius <m>r</m>.
        Find the following values or points on the non-unit circles below
        exactly.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task workspace=".5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In a circle of radius <m>11</m>, find the arc length intercepted by a
          central angle of <m>\frac{5\pi}{3}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>r=11</m>, <m>\theta=\frac{5\pi}{3}</m>, <m>s = ?</m>.  So 
          <m>{s = r\theta = 11 (\frac{5\pi}3) = \frac{55\pi}{3}}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In a circle of radius <m>3</m>, find the central angle measure that
          intercepts an arc of length <m>\frac{\pi}{4}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>r = 3</m>, <m>s =\frac{\pi}{4}</m>, <m>\theta = ?</m>.  So
          <m>{s = r\theta \implies \frac{\pi}{4} = 3\theta \implies \theta = \frac{\pi}{4}(\frac13) \implies \theta = \frac{\pi}{12}}</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the radius of the circle in which an angle of
          <m>\frac{7\pi}{6}</m> intercepts an arc of length
          <m>\frac{\pi}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>r = ?</m>,<m>\theta = \frac{7\pi}{6}</m>, <m>s = \frac{\pi}{4}</m>.  So
          <m>{s = r\theta \implies \frac\pi4 = r (\frac{7\pi}{6}) \implies r = \frac37}</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the exact coordinates of the point on the circle of radius
          <m>5</m> that lies <m>\frac{25\pi}{6}</m> units counterclockwise along
          the circle from <m>(5,0)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution component="S2-1-soln">
        <p>
          <m>r = 5</m>, <m>s = \frac{25\pi}{6}</m>, <m>\theta = ?</m>.  So 
          <m>{s = r\theta \implies \frac{25\pi}{6} = 5\theta \implies \theta = \frac{5\pi}{6}}</m>. 
          So on the unit circle the coordinates corresponding to that angle are 
          <m>{(-\frac{\sqrt3}2,\frac12)}</m>.  But we are on a circle of 
          radius 5, so the coordinate is 5 times bigger, i.e. 
          <m>{(-5\frac{\sqrt3}2,\frac52)}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </activity>
</handout>